# Drafted by Gemini (R11c connections_custom), edited.

~ morgan_start
morgan [weary]: Hold on, friend. You have the same blank look I see in my water bucket every morning.
Narrator: A folded paper scrap rustles between {name}'s fingers, smelling of damp wool and pressed lavender.
morgan [neutral]: Like you woke in the heather with empty pockets and your name feeling like a borrowed coat.
* Hum the three notes that keep returning to you -> morgan_song
* Ask how long they have lived without a past -> morgan_blank
* Say your past is none of their concern -> morgan_quiet

~ morgan_song
Player [weary]: It goes low, then catches on the third note. Like a cradle song with the middle torn out.
morgan [smile]: Da-dum, then the minor drop? Saints above, I have whistled that second half by the hearth for three winters.
Narrator: For the space of a breath, the two halves fit together like a cracked plate mended with warm wax.
-> morgan_pamphlet

~ morgan_blank
morgan [sad]: Two years next tolling. Folks here are kind if you pull your weight and never ask where the road leads.
Player [neutral]: And you never found a single thread to pull?
-> morgan_pamphlet

~ morgan_quiet
morgan [neutral]: I will not pry. When your head holds only mist, even mist feels like private property.
-> morgan_pamphlet

~ morgan_pamphlet
morgan [sly]: Look at this handbill from a salt crate. It speaks of Memory's Rest, a house where the forgetful sit.
-> morgan_close

~ morgan_close
morgan [smile]: Keep listening for the rest of that tune, friend. If you learn who we were, save me a verse.
set connection_apocalypse
quest connection_apocalypse met
attitude morgan friendly
-> END

~ baltus_start
baltus_tolliver [smile]: Pardon, traveler, but I smell coal smoke, cold iron, and river fog on your hem.
Narrator: He tips his hat with a gloved hand, a brass ticket punch chiming in his waistcoat pocket.
baltus_tolliver [sly]: Only one engine in Druskenvald burns that fuel. You have ridden the Ghostlight Express before tonight, yes?
* Admit you remember the whistle and the green lamps -> baltus_train
* Ask what a village merchant knows of that train -> baltus_inquiry
* Step back and tell him he is mistaken -> baltus_deny

~ baltus_train
Player [neutral]: I know those wheels on the trestle. After that, ordinary iron sounds dead.
baltus_tolliver [smile]: Quite so. A passenger of distinction never truly leaves the platform, however far inland they wander.
-> baltus_river

~ baltus_inquiry
baltus_tolliver [neutral]: A merchant watches the timetables, friend. Freight moves by rail, cart, and darker conveyances when the moon allows.
-> baltus_river

~ baltus_deny
baltus_tolliver [smile]: My mistake entirely. The moorland damp plays curious tricks on velvet and wool.
-> baltus_river

~ baltus_river
baltus_tolliver [sly]: Should you long for that furnace heat again, look downriver. The Stranger keeps another engine churning inside a paddlewheel hull.
Narrator: Lantern light catches his spectacles, turning his eyes into flat coins of yellow glass.
-> baltus_close

~ baltus_close
baltus_tolliver [neutral]: Call on my counter when your purse is heavy. We understand one another, and I favor repeat travelers.
set connection_deliverance
quest connection_deliverance met
attitude baltus_tolliver friendly
-> END

~ finneas_start
finneas_trout [afraid]: Do not move your boots. Stand right where the lantern hits the gravel, if you please.
Narrator: The schoolmaster squints past {name} at the flagstones, where {name}'s shadow lags a thumb's breadth behind the step.
finneas_trout [weary]: Merciful heaven, there are two of you: you, and the piece that slipped its leash.
* Ask where he has seen your likeness before -> finneas_double
* Ask what he means by a piece slipping loose -> finneas_grey
* Tell him to mind his primers and chalk -> finneas_warn

~ finneas_double
finneas_trout [afraid]: Three nights before the sheepfold butcherings, someone with your stride passed my schoolhouse heading north-east, without lantern or footfall on the frost.
Player [neutral]: If it wore my face, it did not ask my leave to borrow it.
-> finneas_grey

~ finneas_warn
finneas_trout [neutral]: Scold me if you must, but a counting teacher knows when one and one make a wrong number.
-> finneas_grey

~ finneas_grey
finneas_trout [sly]: My notes on the old monographs call it the Grey: a soul entering Druskenvald crookedly divides meat from reflection at the threshold.
Narrator: Chalk dust puffs from his cuff as he traces a split circle in the air between you.
-> finneas_close

~ finneas_close
finneas_trout [weary]: Watch the puddles and dark panes, traveler. Whatever walked north-east toward the hills is practicing your handwriting.
set connection_duality
quest connection_duality met
attitude finneas_trout friendly
-> END

~ jean_start
jean_myriel [neutral]: Turn your wrist over, stranger. No, the left one, where the cuff is frayed.
Narrator: Forge heat rolls off behind him, sharp with quenched steel and old winter sweat.
jean_myriel [weary]: Only a Crimson aspirant holds a blade thumb-high like a lancet, waiting for the vein to rise.
* Show him the old scar along your palm -> jean_blood
* Ask where a village smith learned the Rose's grip -> jean_past
* Pull your hand back and say nothing -> jean_silent

~ jean_blood
Player [neutral]: The ink faded in the mist, but the calluses stayed. We learned iron stays thirsty until it drinks.
jean_myriel [sad]: Aye, and waking the metal leaves it thirsty forever. I folded enough red steel before laying down the tongs.
-> jean_monastery

~ jean_past
jean_myriel [weary]: I wore the briar cord twenty years back, tempering three swords in warm basins before seeing what the steel took from me.
-> jean_monastery

~ jean_silent
jean_myriel [neutral]: Keep your secrets. An old anvil knows the hammer's weight whether the smith speaks or not.
-> jean_monastery

~ jean_monastery
jean_myriel [angry]: If you seek the brothers at Dawn's Gate, turn back to the valley. That monastery is a slaughterhouse in stained glass.
-> jean_close

~ jean_close
jean_myriel [neutral]: In Wickermoor, I mend plowshares and horseshoes, and nobody bleeds for a hinge. Come by for honest iron.
set connection_immortality
quest connection_immortality met
attitude jean_myriel friendly
-> END

~ solomon_start
solomon_roderick [angry]: Keep your hands in the torchlight, stranger. You stoop like someone used to low lintels.
Narrator: Stone dust coats the mason's knuckles as his chisel taps a dry warning against his leather apron.
solomon_roderick [weary]: Every night since the frost, a crooked man at the foot of my bed bends his neck that exact way.
* Tell him you left the circle and broke your oath -> solomon_dream
* Ask what else he sees in those nightmares -> solomon_coven
* Say a stiff neck is no crime in a damp country -> solomon_stone

~ solomon_dream
Player [weary]: I knelt in the ashes once, mason, before I knew what waited behind the branches. I am done kneeling.
solomon_roderick [neutral]: Easy to say by lantern light. Harder when the thatch whispers at three in the morning.
-> solomon_coven

~ solomon_stone
solomon_roderick [sly]: Damp gets in the knees, not the soul. You know the old litany, do not lie to a headstone cutter.
-> solomon_coven

~ solomon_coven
solomon_roderick [afraid]: In the dream, the Crooked Man counts his servants. Three names belong to the timber coven, their sign carved under my eaves.
Narrator: He rubs his thumb across a scarred groove on his forearm, his breathing slow and gravelly.
-> solomon_close

~ solomon_close
solomon_roderick [neutral]: A runaway hound is best for hunting the pack. Meet me by the yard when the square clears to trade names.
set connection_kindred
quest connection_kindred met
attitude solomon_roderick friendly
-> END

~ eleanor_start
eleanor_heyling [sly]: Do not rub your eyes, dear. Printer's gall and graveyard tallow leave a very specific stain.
Narrator: She peers over half-moon spectacles, smelling of dry vellum, cloves, and mouse-chewed linen.
eleanor_heyling [smile]: You have the uncut folio sickness. Someone has been reading chapters meant to stay sewn shut.
* Recite the third footnote from the Verdigris Concord -> eleanor_cite
* Ask if she carries anything worth the headache -> eleanor_places
* Claim it is only lack of sleep from the road -> eleanor_sickness

~ eleanor_cite
Player [sly]: "Wherein the seventh bell is cast without a tongue, lest the parish hear the metal confess." Second edition, bound in salted eel-skin.
eleanor_heyling [smile]: Delightful! Though the third edition corrects "parish" to "abbey" just before the censor's fire took the press.
-> eleanor_places

~ eleanor_sickness
eleanor_heyling [neutral]: Road dust washes off. A crooked footnote stays behind the brow-bone, ticking like a beetle in the wall.
-> eleanor_places

~ eleanor_places
eleanor_heyling [sly]: I keep mostly almanacs and pastorals, for my health. What you crave is split between an island, a sunken library, and a dead bailiff's ledger.
Narrator: Her fingernail traces the gilt spine of a ledger on her counter, leaving a thin line in the dust.
-> eleanor_close

~ eleanor_close
eleanor_heyling [smile]: Come back with a quiet hour and a dry candle. Few readers know books can bite.
set connection_malediction
quest connection_malediction met
attitude eleanor_heyling friendly
-> END

~ weston_start
weston_murdoch [smile]: Bless the road! Look at that cloak, neighbors, provincial wool if ever I saw a loom.
Narrator: The preacher's voice rings warm as spiced cider across the cobbles, his hands ungloved.
weston_murdoch [neutral]: You know the crooked moon, friend, though you are new to Wickermoor. Which province claimed your prayers?
* Sing him a harvesting round from the outer parishes -> weston_song
* Tell him your home kept candles for every altar -> weston_faiths
* Say one dark sky looks much like another -> weston_province

~ weston_song
Player [smile]: "A sheaf for the rain, a stone for the gate, and a song for the bird that wakes too late." We sang it when wagons came in whole.
weston_murdoch [smile]: Ha! Three faiths braided into four bars of music, without a sour note.
-> weston_close

~ weston_faiths
weston_murdoch [smile]: The grand old provincial way. Oak bough over the barn, sunflower on the table, songbird on the cradle.
Player [neutral]: Folks here in the village seem to prefer choosing a single fence and standing behind it.
-> weston_close

~ weston_province
weston_murdoch [neutral]: Thin boots make cynics of us all, yet your tongue keeps the rhythm of the old parish bells.
-> weston_close

~ weston_close
weston_murdoch [smile]: Our choir needs a voice that knows Druskenvald is wider than one square. Walk in grace, cousin, my door is unlatched.
set connection_rapture
quest connection_rapture met
attitude weston_murdoch friendly
-> END
